          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-7122280fbf634f60\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Lorck’s “Jeg elskede dig, Conny” (“I loved you, Conny”) is a direct retrospective avowal presenting his past love as true. It does not independently undertake or renew a commitment, and nothing indicates that the words function as a reflex-like affective discharge rather than an affectionate assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The intimate marital setting gives the avowal a reassuring relational effect, but that effect does not by itself establish performative force. The past tense may invite an inference about continuing love, though the immediate emphasis is on the history of his feelings.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Lorck directly addresses Constance in spoken dialogue: “Jeg elskede dig, Conny.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lorck says this while the couple recall his courtship and Constance’s former reluctance. His subsequent claim that he had never loved before loving her reinforces the utterance as a retrospective avowal of genuine, uniquely significant love. Although the scene is physically tender and the statement helps reassure Constance, neither tenderness nor reassurance supplies independent expressive/reflexive or commitment-making force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg elskede dig, Conny.”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person affirmative statement directly presents Lorck’s past love for Constance as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"E2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lorck continues: “som sagt, jeg elskede dig, jeg har aldrig elsket, før jeg begyndte at elske dig” (“as I said, I loved you; I had never loved before I began to love you”).\",\"supports\":\"His explicit repetition and autobiographical comparison frame the target as a considered account of his emotional history rather than a reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"E3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The utterance follows discussion of Lorck’s perseverance during courtship and Constance’s initial reluctance.\",\"supports\":\"The conversational role is retrospective explanation and reassurance, with no explicit undertaking or promise that would establish P.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"E4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is annotated as affirmative, past tense/aspect, with the valid explicit-love membership decision.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified form is an explicit past-tense love predication rather than the later present-tense utterance by Constance.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core retrospective avowal. The passage’s tenderness, reassurance, and marital history are contextual effects adequately described without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The target is Lorck’s past-tense utterance, not Constance’s later repeated “Jeg elsker dig.”\"}"
